But wait, actually looking at the logic: if `end` is after all ranges, that means we want to read from beginning to line `end`, but we've only read up to `last_rng.end`. So we need to read lines `last_rng.end+1` to `end`. Actually, we also need to check if we've read lines 1 to `last_rng.end` completely. If there are gaps in the merged ranges, we might not have read everything up to `last_rng.end`.

